<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- net48 cannot RUN on the Linux CI runner, and `dotnet test` runs every TFM it finds. The
         OS condition keeps the Linux legs at net8.0 exactly as before; on Windows the solution
         builds and runs both. The cost, stated: Linux CI no longer COMPILES the net48 leg, so a
         net48 portability regression surfaces only on Windows. -->
    <TargetFrameworks Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))">net8.0;net48</TargetFrameworks>
    <TargetFrameworks Condition="!$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))">net8.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net48'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <!-- ZipArchive, for the test that builds an OPC package whose parts are binary (FormulaAbsenceTests).
         The SDK's implicit .NET Framework references stop at System.IO.Compression.FileSystem, and a
         dependency of a reference is not itself a reference to the compiler, so on this leg the type
         has to be asked for by name. It is inbox on net48; nothing is added to the package graph. -->
    <Reference Include="System.IO.Compression" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Unrect\Unrect.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Unrect.Core\Unrect.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Unrect.Strategies\Unrect.Strategies.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Unrect.Spreadsheets\Unrect.Spreadsheets.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The example workbooks are copied into the test project rather than referenced out of
    ..\..\examples so the tests are path-independent: they resolve their inputs relative to
    the test assembly's own output directory.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="TestData\simple-report.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="TestData\investors-by-deal.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="TestData\investor-summary.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="TestData\edge-cases.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="TestData\investor-irr.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The two streaming fixtures. Unlike the workbooks above they are not example files with a
    script behind them; they are synthetic and exist only to be read, so they live here and not
    in ..\..\examples. multi-sheet.xlsx has three sheets with different shapes, so the catalogue
    walk has something to walk past. tall-ledger.xlsx has 1,200 data rows, enough that a window
    of one chunk genuinely evicts — every other fixture is short enough to floor at the
    four-chunk minimum, where nothing is ever dropped.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="TestData\multi-sheet.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="TestData\tall-ledger.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The sheet that will not say how big it is: four ragged rows of formatted, valueless cells,
    with the dimension element stripped. The missing dimension alone would not do it — ExcelDataReader
    pre-scans the cells on every format and derives its counts from them — so what makes the reader
    report neither a row count nor a field count is that no cell carries a VALUE for the pre-scan to
    find. Both halves are load-bearing: a regenerated fixture that keeps a value anywhere describes
    itself again and stops reaching the path these tests are about.
    It is the one committed file that reaches the measure-by-reading path in either door, and it
    exists to hold both doors to the same extent over it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="TestData\no-extent.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The workbook that exists to have duplicated text in it, for the tests that pin equal Text cells
    onto one instance of their characters. Two properties are load-bearing, and a regenerated file
    that loses either stops testing anything:
      1. Every text cell is spelled INLINE (t="inlineStr"), so the reader hands the adapter a FRESH
         instance per cell. A shared-string fixture comes back deduplicated by the reader itself
         and would say nothing about the adapter's own table.
      2. The duplicates are deliberate, and include the length guard's two neighbours: a value of
         exactly 256 characters, which is shared, and one of 257, which is never entered.
    Two sheets ('Ledger' and 'Notes') carry values in common, so sharing can be shown ACROSS the
    sheets of one Create call and one Workbook rather than only within one sheet. Every other
    committed fixture is written inline too and several happen to repeat a value, but they repeat it
    incidentally — none of them would be regenerated with these properties in mind.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="TestData\repeated-text.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The formula fixture, and the one file here whose sheetData is hand-written: openpyxl spells
    every formula out in full, which is exactly the case that needs no reconstruction. What is
    load-bearing, and what a regenerated file must keep:
      1. D2:D5 is a SHARED group whose master text exercises every rule the shifter has —
         IF(B2>0,ROUND(B2*$C$2,2)+SUM($B$2:B2),"B2"): a relative reference that must move, two
         absolute ones that must not, a "B2" inside a string literal that must not, and three
         function names, since a name followed by ( is never a reference.
      2. B9:D9 is a second shared group shifting COLUMNS rather than rows, and its text is
         LOG10(B8)+B8 — LOG10 is the classic false reference (column LOG, row 10).
      3. B12 is an ARRAY anchor over B12:C12 whose spilled cell C12 carries a value and no <f>,
         which is what the reader must report as null.
      4. B14 is =TEXT(6*7,"0") cached as the STRING "42": the cell's kind and its formula's shape
         disagree, so a test can tell reading a formula from reading a value.
    The generator is docs-only scratchpad work; the file is the artefact.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="TestData\formulas.xlsx">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The legacy workbook: the only .xls in the tree, and the only file that can state the .xls
    absence — that asking a BIFF workbook for its formulas fails loudly rather than reading none.
    What is load-bearing:
      1. It is a real BIFF8 file (written by xlwt), not an .xlsx under another extension. The
         refusal it triggers comes from the file NOT being a zip, so a renamed xlsx would take a
         different path and prove nothing.
      2. B3 carries a genuine =SUM(B1:B2), so the refusal is about a capability this reader does
         not have rather than about a file with nothing to read.
      3. Its two data rows are ordinary text and numbers, because the other half of the pin is that
         the plain door reads this file perfectly well.
    Recorded because it is surprising: B3's VALUE reads Blank, since the writer stores no cached
    result for its formula and ExcelDataReader has none to report. That is the fixture's property,
    not the adapter's, and no test asserts on that cell.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="TestData\legacy.xls">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
